1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,326
Good morning.

2
00:00:01,326 --> 00:00:05,613
I'm very pleased to have been invited along to your club to talk about

3
00:00:05,613 --> 00:00:08,420
our Charity Sponsored Walking Holiday for Education Aid.

4
00:00:08,420 --> 00:00:12,510
I'll start by giving you a brief overview of what it entails.

5
00:00:12,510 --> 00:00:16,440
First of all let me explain what we mean by 'sponsored' here.

6
00:00:16,440 --> 00:00:20,726
This is where people promise to donate money to the charity if you achieve

7
00:00:20,726 --> 00:00:24,400
your goal, in this case to walk a certain number of miles.

8
00:00:24,400 --> 00:00:30,370
Basically we are organizing a ten-day holiday, from the sixth to the sixteenth of

9
00:00:30,370 --> 00:00:35,060
November, with eight days actual walking, trekking in the Semira Mountains.

10
00:00:35,060 --> 00:00:38,280
Let's have a look at some of the details.

11
00:00:38,280 --> 00:00:44,735
We require you to raise sponsorship money of at least $3,200, paying $250 of

12
00:00:44,735 --> 00:00:50,760
it up front as a deposit and the rest in stages throughout the year.

13
00:00:50,760 --> 00:00:55,255
Out of this about thirty-five per cent will go on your expenses,

14
00:00:55,255 --> 00:00:59,750
and that leaves sixty-five per cent guaranteed to go to the charity.

15
00:00:59,750 --> 00:01:02,840
Which brings me to the most important part.

16
00:01:02,840 --> 00:01:08,200
This trek is being specifically organized to help education in the Semira region.

17
00:01:08,200 --> 00:01:11,567
Last year we helped train teachers for the disabled,

18
00:01:11,567 --> 00:01:14,560
and this year we're focusing on the pupils.

19
00:01:14,560 --> 00:01:19,673
Each of the walkers' sponsorship money will go to help an individual special needs

20
00:01:19,673 --> 00:01:22,230
pupil in one of the mountain schools.

21
00:01:22,230 --> 00:01:26,663
In the second part of the talk I'll be giving you a lot more

22
00:01:26,663 --> 00:01:26,980
details,

23
00:01:26,980 --> 00:01:28,880
but back to the basic information.

24
00:01:28,880 --> 00:01:29,621
Age limits.

25
00:01:29,621 --> 00:01:34,068
This is the second time we have run this kind of holiday

26
00:01:34,068 --> 00:01:38,144
and um, on the first we even had an eighty-year old,

27
00:01:38,144 --> 00:01:42,220
but we found it was wise to establish limits this time.

28
00:01:42,220 --> 00:01:46,639
You have to be at least eighteen and the top limit is now seventy,

29
00:01:46,639 --> 00:01:51,057
though you need to obtain a health certificate from your doctor if you are

30
00:01:51,057 --> 00:01:52,320
over sixty years old.

31
00:01:52,320 --> 00:01:56,115
Now, the Semira Mountains are among the highest in the world

32
00:01:56,115 --> 00:02:00,945
but you mustn't be too daunted, we will mainly be trekking in the foothills

33
00:02:00,945 --> 00:02:04,740
only, although there will be spectacular views even in the foothills.

34
00:02:04,740 --> 00:02:08,864
However, you will need to be extremely fit if you aren't now

35
00:02:08,864 --> 00:02:11,270
and you're interested in coming with us.

36
00:02:11,270 --> 00:02:13,740
You have plenty of time to get into shape.

37
00:02:13,740 --> 00:02:19,513
You will be sleeping in tents so you must have quite a bit of equipment with you

38
00:02:19,513 --> 00:02:22,230
but you will be helped by local assistants.

39
00:02:22,230 --> 00:02:25,120
Your bedding and so forth will be carried by them.

40
00:02:25,120 --> 00:02:29,507
We ask that you only walk with a small rucksack with needs for the

41
00:02:29,507 --> 00:02:29,820
day.

42
00:02:29,820 --> 00:02:34,711
I don't think I've really said enough about the marvellous area you'll be walking

43
00:02:34,711 --> 00:02:35,060
in.

44
00:02:35,060 --> 00:02:37,970
Let's have a look at some of the sights you'll be seeing.

45
00:02:37,970 --> 00:02:44,820
Apart from these spectacular snow-covered peaks and valleys, there are marvellous historic villages.

46
00:02:44,820 --> 00:02:49,596
The area has been famous for centuries for making beautiful carpets, although recently there

47
00:02:49,596 --> 00:02:53,690
has been a trend to move into weaving blankets and wood carving.

48
00:02:53,690 --> 00:02:56,750
The people are extremely friendly and welcoming.

49
00:02:56,750 --> 00:03:02,132
We deliberately keep the parties small in size to minimize disruption to people

50
00:03:02,132 --> 00:03:02,960
and landscape.

51
00:03:02,960 --> 00:03:05,570
I hope that there are still some people interested.

52
00:03:05,570 --> 00:03:10,217
I will be distributing leaflets at the end where you can find out more

53
00:03:10,217 --> 00:03:10,549
information,

54
00:03:10,549 --> 00:03:15,196
but just for the moment I'll outline the itinerary, the main high points of

55
00:03:15,196 --> 00:03:15,860
the holiday.

56
00:03:15,860 --> 00:03:21,000
Obviously, you'll start by flying out to Kishba, the capital city, on Day One.

57
00:03:21,000 --> 00:03:25,634
After a couple of days to acclimatize yourself, you'll start the trek on Day

58
00:03:25,634 --> 00:03:30,269
Three walking through the enormous Katiba Forest which will take the whole of the

59
00:03:30,269 --> 00:03:30,600
day.

60
00:03:30,600 --> 00:03:35,525
Day Four takes us higher up, going through the foothills past a number of villages

61
00:03:35,525 --> 00:03:38,480
and visiting a school for the disabled in Sohan.

62
00:03:38,480 --> 00:03:40,842
Then you have a rest day,

63
00:03:40,842 --> 00:03:46,354
that's Day Five, before going to the spectacular Kumi Temple with twelfth-century carvings, set

64
00:03:46,354 --> 00:03:51,866
in a small forest by a lake and that's Day Six, the highlight for

65
00:03:51,866 --> 00:03:52,260
many.

66
00:03:52,260 --> 00:03:57,621
We stay near there for Day Seven because then comes the hardest day, walking

67
00:03:57,621 --> 00:03:59,153
through very mountainous country,

68
00:03:59,153 --> 00:04:02,600
but culminating in a swim in the Parteh Falls.

69
00:04:02,600 --> 00:04:05,052
This is the highest waterfall in the region.

70
00:04:05,052 --> 00:04:09,342
Day Nine is much easier, with part of the day spent in a village

71
00:04:09,342 --> 00:04:12,100
where they make some of the gorgeous red blankets.

72
00:04:12,100 --> 00:04:15,540
Then back down to Kishba and the journey home.

73
00:04:15,540 --> 00:04:19,550
So you can see it's a pretty packed timetable...
